ing  desperate because when you a desperate  investor you have a property that's  empty and you become desperate  you can move bad tenants in so if you've  got an empty property I would say always  hold out for a good tenant never move  somebody in in a rush so the things you  have to do to bet them is number one I  now I'm not discriminating against  people that don't work it might be that  your niche is students or DSS tenants  but personally my properties are pretty  much all professional properties so I'm  looking for can they afford to pay the  rent if they can't have they got a  guarantor you can afford to pay the rent  so come in for it number two I want to  see a previous landlord check I want to  see a reference now something that I do  personally is rather than getting a  landlord reference from their previous  landlord I don't do that I get a  previous previous landlord check and the  reason is is because it might be that  their previous landlord ie their  existing landlord who will be previous  when they move hates their guts and is  desperate to get rid of them because  they're a terrible tenant and what  happens is sometimes a landlord will  give a good reference just because he  wants them to move on because he can't  wait to get rid of them so as more  accurate reference will come from a  previous to the previous so go back  maybe a few years and asked the tenant  to get a previous previous language  check also credit check had they ever  not paid for things before have they got  a good credit get a work reference so  when they say I'm working for this  company ask for a reference do they work  here another good member of staff so  when you've got these kind of references  credit checks potential guarantor it's  getting massively massively take the  risk out of the tenant not paying any  rent so if you do these things it should  ensure that your tenant pays the rent so  you're pretty safe now how do you make  sure that the tenant doesn't trash the  p